WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.967
A relational database executes a SQL
program and evaluates data values within that

2
00:00:05.047 --> 00:00:05.557
program.

3
00:00:05.637 --> 00:00:10.232
SQL injection becomes possible when
externally controlled data is used to construct

4
00:00:10.312 --> 00:00:11.397
the program itself.

5
00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:15.944
In TypeScript, the dangerous boundary is
easy to cross with string concatenation,

6
00:00:16.024 --> 00:00:20.555
template-literal interpolation, or a
helper that accepts a complete SQL fragment.

7
00:00:20.635 --> 00:00:24.846
A route that inserts a request email
directly between quote characters has not

8
00:00:24.926 --> 00:00:26.713
merely mishandled punctuation.

9
00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:30.750
It has allowed request data to influence
where SQL syntax begins and ends.

10
00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:34.148
The same defect can appear outside a
simple WHERE comparison.

11
00:00:34.228 --> 00:00:38.503
Applications dynamically assemble filters,
sort clauses, selected columns,

12
00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:42.285
table names, reporting expressions, and
administrative commands.

13
00:00:42.365 --> 00:00:45.683
If raw request text enters any of those
structural positions,

14
00:00:45.763 --> 00:00:49.081
the database may interpret part of that
text as program logic.

15
00:00:49.161 --> 00:00:52.160
The consequences can affect
confidentiality, writes,

16
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.005
authentication decisions, reporting
accuracy, and data integrity.

17
00:00:56.085 --> 00:01:00.425
A read-only endpoint is not automatically
low risk when its database account can

18
00:01:00.505 --> 00:01:01.717
reach sensitive data.

19
00:01:01.797 --> 00:01:06.009
Browser validation cannot defend this
boundary because a client can send HTTP

20
00:01:06.089 --> 00:01:06.663
directly.

21
00:01:06.743 --> 00:01:09.232
Authentication also does not make input
safe;

22
00:01:09.312 --> 00:01:13.715
an authenticated user can submit
unexpected data and may be limited to only part of

23
00:01:13.795 --> 00:01:14.816
a shared database.

24
00:01:14.896 --> 00:01:17.066
TypeScript types improve development,

25
00:01:17.146 --> 00:01:21.421
but an external string remains a runtime
string even if code asserts that it has a

26
00:01:21.501 --> 00:01:22.586
trusted interface.

27
00:01:22.666 --> 00:01:26.048
None of these controls separates the value
from the SQL parser.

28
00:01:26.128 --> 00:01:30.468
The durable design rule is that trusted
application code authors SQL structure,

29
00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:34.313
while external values are bound separately
through the database API.

30
00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:37.839
This keeps a value in a data position
regardless of its contents.

31
00:01:37.919 --> 00:01:42.641
Structural choices that cannot be ordinary
parameters require a different treatment:

32
00:01:42.721 --> 00:01:46.039
the server maps a limited client choice to
a trusted fragment.

33
00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:48.608
Secure review therefore follows
provenance.

34
00:01:48.688 --> 00:01:53.091
Identify which text forms the SQL program,
determine where each part originated,

35
00:01:53.171 --> 00:01:57.510
and ensure that untrusted values never
become executable syntax through string

36
00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:58.420
construction.
